 you know eight  months  absolutely  so the first thing i'm going to say is  that mental health is more important  than school  progress  so  the  first thing i would say is  think about  as you said dan some amount of just  being  mildly depressed and a little bit  anxious is pretty normal in these these  kinds of times  if you're finding that it seems to be  beyond a little bit if it's really  interfering with their interactions with  your family if it's really interfering  with their just daily lives like they  are not getting out of bed um you know  real those kinds of signs  i i absolutely recommend professional  help um  you know talk to your pediatrician is a  good starting point make sure to you  know think about what are the signs of  you know someone who's who's needing  who for whom this is a little bit more  than oh this is tough um  so that's the first thing i would say is  know when to get help um there's  a number you know lots of good ways to  check it but the as a what psychologists  usually look for is when it starts to  interfere with your daily life as kind  of a good rule of thumb for when it  makes sense to get help  but if it is like less than that and  it's hey you know i just am sad today  i'm just feeling anxious today  all of those things so first as a parent  just empathize  just like dan just did hey you know what  i get it this situation is no fun like  all of us are struggling and i it is  totally okay to struggle um i also  encourage  you know the way this was written was  being in the house it does help if  you're in a place even if it's  um not with other people to at least get  some  outside air to go out and and move and  um get some get some fresh air if that's  possible where you live and in your  weather and all those kinds of things um  the next piece is that exercise piece  like moving can be important and can  help kids get up so even if they're if  you're thinking about the school day  they should have breaks in their school  day where they are getting up and moving  and walking and and i i'm saying this as  do as i say not as i do i'm the worst  that i've been sitting in my chair here  for like eight hours um but thinking  about getting up and moving is okay  and you know making sure some of that's  built in and the final thing that teens  really struggle with i think is the peer  interaction and the  the reduced care interaction they've had  in these times so if there are ways that  you can help facilitate  um whether that's online  you know video interactions whether  that's um if you have you know a small  safe group um i know some people are  doing hey there's two other families  that we all you know  have kind of the same view on what's  acceptable in terms of safety and um and  we all get together you know finding  some ways that kids can connect with  other kids  can be helpful too in these times that  we found so  lots of different potential things but  um  empathize definitely you know talk to  your kids about it  make try to keep those doors open so  that they can talk to you when they have  concerns  great advice thanks kristin  um we have a question from um barbara  who's asking advice for kids who strive  for perfection who are super motivated  but they are also um they kind of are  worried about doing things that are  imperfect and that experience in itself  overwhelms them um so how do how do you  help those students um i think some of  the compartmentalizing and making things  more chunkable that you talked about  earlier was super super relevant for  this type of case as well  yeah definitely thinking about um  how to you know  think about what is  uh what is done what is acceptable on  for an assignment um what is okay and  the other piece is to really try to  focus on  learning as opposed to grades so one of  the things that often happens with kids  who kind of have that perfectionist  streak is they really start to focus on  getting a hundred or you know getting  